+<body>
+<script>
+/* Reaper-native page: standalone hits get the shell chrome (fragment passes the shell allow-list) */
+if (self === top) location.href = '/reaper_shell.asp#Reaper_QoS.asp';
+</script>
+
+<div class="pagehead">
+  <div>
+    <div class="eyebrow">Traffic Manager</div>
+    <h1>Quality of Service</h1>
+  </div>
+  <span class="pill" id="engpill"><span class="dot"></span><span id="engpill_t">Loading</span></span>
+  <div class="spacer"></div>
+  <button type="button" class="btn ghost" onclick="faqOpen()">FAQ / Tuning Guide</button>
+  <button type="button" class="btn primary" onclick="applyQoS()">Apply</button>
+</div>
+
+<div class="panel">
+  <h2>Mode</h2>
+  <div class="sub">Pick how upload/download traffic is shaped. Changing the mode (or turning QoS on) reboots the router so the flow accelerator can be reconfigured; edits within a mode apply live.</div>
+  <div class="modes" id="modes"></div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="panel" id="bwpanel">
+  <h2>Bandwidth</h2>
+  <div class="sub">The shaper must own the bottleneck. Set upload to 90&ndash;95% of what a speed test <i>measures</i> (not the plan rate) &mdash; if this equals or exceeds what the line really delivers, the ISP buffer takes over and QoS barely engages.</div>
+  <div class="bwrow">
+    <div class="bwfield">
+      <label>Upload (obw)</label>
+      <input type="text" id="bw_up" maxlength="7"><span class="unit">Mb/s</span>
+    </div>
+    <div class="bwfield">
+      <label>Download (ibw)</label>
+      <input type="text" id="bw_dn" maxlength="7"><span class="unit">Mb/s</span>
+      <div class="qnote" style="margin-top:5px">Used by Traditional / Bandwidth Limiter / Cake. Hardware QoS shapes upload only.</div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="panel" id="clpanel" style="display:none">
+  <h2>Classes &mdash; priority, guarantees, ceilings</h2>
+  <div class="sub">Top row is served first (strict priority). <b>Min %</b> is a guaranteed floor under load; <b>Ceiling %</b> is the most a class may ever take. With the aggregate cap on, ceilings may total over 100% &mdash; they become burst allowances inside the capped upload.</div>
+
+  <div class="togglerow">
+    <label class="tswitch"><input type="checkbox" id="tg_pshaper"><span class="track"></span></label>
+    <div class="tmeta">
+      <div class="tname">Aggregate cap (port shaper)</div>
+      <div class="tdesc">Holds the whole WAN port at the upload figure above, so combined class traffic can never spill into the ISP buffer &mdash; even when several classes saturate at once. Leave on unless you are debugging.</div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+  <div class="togglerow">
+    <label class="tswitch"><input type="checkbox" id="tg_minrates"><span class="track"></span></label>
+    <div class="tmeta">
+      <div class="tname">Guaranteed minimums</div>
+      <div class="tdesc">Enforces the Min % column as a committed rate per class. Guarantees only matter under contention; they must total 100% or less.</div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+  <div class="togglerow">
+    <label class="tswitch"><input type="checkbox" id="tg_dscp"><span class="track"></span></label>
+    <div class="tmeta">
+      <div class="tname">Trust DSCP marking</div>
+      <div class="tdesc">Maps standard DiffServ classes from LAN devices (EF/CS5+ to Q1, AF4x to Q2, AF3x to Q3, CS1 scavenger to Q5) ahead of the default class. Your explicit rules always win. Off by default: any LAN host can influence its own priority when this is on.</div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <table class="classes">
+    <thead><tr>
+      <th style="width:64px">Order</th><th>Class name</th><th style="width:110px">Queue</th>
+      <th style="width:90px">Min %</th><th style="width:90px">Ceiling %</th>
+      <th style="width:130px">Rate (live)</th><th style="width:110px">Drops</th>
+    </tr></thead>
+    <tbody id="ctbody"></tbody>
+  </table>
+
+  <div class="xq">
+    <div class="xt"><div class="xn">Unclassified (Q0)</div><div class="xv statcell" id="xq0">&ndash;</div></div>
+    <div class="xt"><div class="xn">LAN / Multicast (Q6)</div><div class="xv statcell" id="xq6">&ndash;</div></div>
+    <div class="xt"><div class="xn">AQM</div><div class="xv">PI2 on every queue &mdash; drops rise briefly when a class is held to its rate; that is the system working.</div></div>
+  </div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="panel linksrow" id="linkspanel">
+  <h2>Related pages</h2>
+  <div class="sub" style="margin-bottom:6px">Classification and per-device controls stay on their existing pages:</div>
+  <a href="/Advanced_QOSUserRules_Content.asp">Classification rules (match by IP / MAC / port / protocol)</a> &middot;
+  <a href="/Advanced_QOSUserPrio_Content.asp">Traditional QoS priorities</a> &middot;
+  <a href="/QoS_EZQoS.asp">Stock QoS page (Bandwidth Limiter per-device editor)</a>
+  <div class="hint">Rule of thumb: match on <b>IP, MAC, port or protocol</b> &mdash; not transferred-bytes. Accelerated flows keep the class of their first packet, so byte-count rules misclassify long flows.</div>
+</div>
+
+<!-- FAQ fly-out -->
+<div class="scrim" id="scrim" onclick="faqClose()"></div>
+<aside class="flyout" id="flyout" aria-label="FAQ and tuning guide">
+  <div class="fhead"><h2>FAQ / Tuning Guide</h2><span class="fclose" onclick="faqClose()">&times;</span></div>
+  <div class="fbody">
+    <details open>
+      <summary>Which mode should I pick?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">
+        <ul>
+          <li><b>HW QoS Classful</b> &mdash; the recommended default. Five priority classes, guaranteed minimums, hardware AQM, and the NAT accelerator stays on (full multi-gigabit routing).</li>
+          <li><b>Hardware QoS</b> &mdash; single-queue version: one upload cap + AQM, no classes. Simplest possible bufferbloat fix.</li>
+          <li><b>Cake</b> &mdash; best <i>download</i> bufferbloat control, but it is software: the NAT accelerator turns off and one CPU core does all shaping. Fine below ~1 Gb/s.</li>
+          <li><b>Traditional</b> &mdash; the classic software engine. Accelerator off. Only if you need its exact semantics.</li>
+          <li><b>Bandwidth Limiter</b> &mdash; not bufferbloat control; hard per-device speed caps (guest devices, kids' devices).</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>How do I set the bandwidth figure?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">Run a few speed tests at a quiet hour, take the <b>lowest consistent upload</b>, and enter 90&ndash;95% of it. The point is that <b>this router</b> must be the bottleneck: if the value is at or above what the line truly delivers, queueing happens in the ISP's equipment where nothing here can manage it. Too low just wastes headroom.</div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>Priority vs Minimum vs Ceiling &mdash; what does each do?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">
+        <b>Priority (row order)</b>: under contention the top class is served first, completely. Rank by latency-sensitivity, not importance.<br><br>
+        <b>Min %</b>: a floor the class is guaranteed when the link is saturated. Idle guarantees cost nothing. Mins must total 100% or less &mdash; you cannot promise more than the link.<br><br>
+        <b>Ceiling %</b>: the most the class may take, ever. The ceiling on a <i>high</i>-priority class is what protects everyone below it from starvation &mdash; never give Q1 100%.
+      </div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>Suggested setups</summary>
+      <div class="fc">
+        <b>Work-from-home</b> (calls first):
+        <ul><li>Q1 Web/VoIP &mdash; min 15 / ceil 20</li><li>Q2 Gaming &mdash; min 5 / ceil 10</li><li>Q3 Streaming &mdash; min 10 / ceil 30</li><li>Q4 Default &mdash; min 20 / ceil 50</li><li>Q5 Downloads &mdash; min 10 / ceil 95</li></ul>
+        <b>Gamer</b>: move Gaming to Q1 (min 10 / ceil 15), VoIP Q2.<br>
+        <b>Small office</b>: raise Default's min (most traffic is unclassified there) and keep bulk backup jobs in Q5 with a high ceiling &mdash; bottom priority makes them yield instantly when anyone needs the line.
+      </div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>Why do bulk classes get a HIGH ceiling?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">Strict priority already makes the bottom class yield the moment anything above it wants the line. A high ceiling (95%) simply lets backups saturate an otherwise idle link. Low priority + high ceiling is the correct combination for bulk; a low ceiling would only slow it down at 3 a.m. for no one's benefit.</div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>What does DSCP trust actually do?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">IP packets carry a 6-bit DiffServ field many applications set voluntarily (Teams/Zoom mark voice EF, video AF4x). With the toggle on, marked traffic is classified by that field <b>after</b> your explicit rules and <b>before</b> the default class. It is off by default because any LAN device can mark its own packets &mdash; a torrent client claiming EF would ride Q1. Turn it on when you broadly trust the devices on the LAN (typical WFH/office), keep it off on networks with untrusted clients.</div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>How do I verify it is working?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">Saturate the upload (large cloud backup) and watch the live columns: the bulk class rate should pin near its ceiling, <span class="fx">drops on the bulk queue should tick up</span> (PI2 holding it to rate), and a simultaneous speed test or call should stay responsive. If nothing changes, the bandwidth figure is probably above what the line really delivers.</div>
+    </details>
+    <details>
+      <summary>Why is hardware QoS upload-only?</summary>
+      <div class="fc">The hardware traffic manager sits on the WAN <i>egress</i> (upload) side. Download shaping in hardware is per-port on this platform, which cannot express one aggregate policy for the internet link. If download bufferbloat is your main problem, Cake handles both directions &mdash; at the cost of the NAT accelerator (throughput tops out near 1 Gb/s).</div>
+    </details>
+  </div>
+</aside>
+
+<div class="applying" id="applying"><div class="abox"><h2 id="ap_t">Applying</h2><p id="ap_p">Settings are being applied.</p></div></div>
